TITLE:: SpiralView
summary:: Spiral's display: where one note goes, trip by trip
categories:: Libraries>Spiral
related:: Classes/Spiral, Classes/SpiralPanel, Classes/FxParamView, Classes/FxDevicePanel

DESCRIPTION::
What link::Classes/SpiralPanel:: puts at the top of Spiral's window, with the meters beside it.

The climb: a note at 440 Hz (a5) goes in, and each bar is where the shifter has put it after one more trip round the loop, as long as a trip takes and as bright as the feedback leaves it. Hz go up on a log scale and seconds along, so the slope is the shift and the length of the train is the feedback. The lo pass (code::damp::) and hi pass (code::loCut::) are walls at the top and bottom, the shaded parts beyond them where a climbing or falling note dies out. The dashed end line is where the train has died out (-60 dB), or says "goes on" when the loop holds it up.

With code::sides:: the right side's trail is hollow; with code::ring:: every trip goes up and down at once; with code::driftDepth:: an orange band is how far the trail wanders. A short loop with a small shift is a comb whose teeth slide, and the caption says so. Behind it all, at the left edge, the output's spectrum lies on its side; while the window is running the trail sways with the drift the server reports.

Under the climb, the shift strip: the shift on its knob's scale, 0 in the middle, down to the left and up to the right, with the left side's needle filled and the right side's hollow.

table::
## strong::in the display:: || strong::does::
## drag the climb || up and down: code::shift::; along: the trips stretch (code::delay::) - the time axis holds still while you drag
## drag the end line || code::feedback::: where the train dies out
## drag a wall || code::damp:: (top) or code::loCut:: (bottom)
## drag the strip || code::shift::, the needle follows the pointer and 0 sticks in the middle
## drag the hollow needle || code::sides::
## the wheel || code::feedback::; on the strip code::shift::, on a wall that wall
## shift || fine, a tenth as far
## double click || back to the default of what is under it
::

It draws from the object's settings and the replies its meters bring, so it is only live while the window is running; link::Classes/FxParamView:: has the rest. The model is in class methods, so a check or a page of notes can ask it the same numbers:

code::
SpiralView.journey(440, 100, 0.5, trips: 3).collect(_[1]);	// [540, 640, 740]
SpiralView.journey(440, -100, 0.5, trips: 6).collect(_[1]);	// [340, 240, 140, 40, 60, 40]: through 0 Hz it folds back up
SpiralView.endTime(24, 0.92, 0.12, 0, 8000, 40);			// the barber pole dies out after about 10 s
SpiralView.feedbackFor(5, 24, 0.12, 0, 8000, 40);			// the feedback for 5 s
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: unit
The link::Classes/Spiral::.
ARGUMENT:: parent
A view to go in.

METHOD:: note
The note the display follows, in Hz (440).

METHOD:: maxTrips
How many trips it follows at most (256): past them a train "goes on".

METHOD:: journey
Where a note goes: an Array of code::[trip, Hz, amplitude]::. Trip 0 is the first pass, at once; each after it a code::delay:: later. It stops when the note is under -60 dB.
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: hz
The shift.
ARGUMENT:: feedback
ARGUMENT:: drive
In dB: the loop's tanh holds a quiet signal up by its square root.
ARGUMENT:: damp
ARGUMENT:: loCut
ARGUMENT:: trips
At most this many (code::maxTrips::).

METHOD:: ringTrips
The same with code::ring::: an Array, a trip each, of code::[Hz, amplitude]:: pairs, until all of a trip's partials together are under -60 dB.
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: hz
ARGUMENT:: ring
ARGUMENT:: feedback
ARGUMENT:: drive
ARGUMENT:: damp
ARGUMENT:: loCut
ARGUMENT:: trips

METHOD:: wall
What the loop's lo pass and hi pass leave of a partial (each 12 dB an octave).
ARGUMENT:: f
ARGUMENT:: damp
ARGUMENT:: loCut

METHOD:: loopGain
A trip's gain at low level: code::feedback * sqrt(drive.dbamp)::.
ARGUMENT:: feedback
ARGUMENT:: drive

METHOD:: goesOn
True when a journey still sounds after code::maxTrips::.
ARGUMENT:: journey
ARGUMENT:: feedback

METHOD:: endTime
When the train has died out, in seconds.
ARGUMENT:: hz
ARGUMENT:: feedback
ARGUMENT:: delay
ARGUMENT:: drive
ARGUMENT:: damp
ARGUMENT:: loCut
ARGUMENT:: ring

METHOD:: feedbackFor
The feedback (0 .. 1) whose train dies out at code::t:: seconds: what dragging the end line sets.
ARGUMENT:: t
ARGUMENT:: hz
ARGUMENT:: delay
ARGUMENT:: drive
ARGUMENT:: damp
ARGUMENT:: loCut
ARGUMENT:: ring

METHOD:: hzText
A shift as the window writes it, with its sign: "+24.0 Hz", "-1.50 kHz", "0 Hz".
ARGUMENT:: v
ARGUMENT:: signed

METHOD:: zoneName
What a shift that size does to a held sound: "phase" (under 2 Hz), "beat" (under 20), "metal" (under 200), "clang".
ARGUMENT:: hz

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: panel
The link::Classes/SpiralPanel:: it is in, so hovering a part of the display lights the knobs that set it.

METHOD:: hot
The parameter whose knob the pointer is on, lit in the display; nil for none.

METHOD:: hint
What the hint bar says over it: what is under the pointer, its value and what dragging does.
ARGUMENT:: x
ARGUMENT:: y
